{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.PolynomialShadowRigidity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PolynomialShadowRigidity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Log","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.RootsOfUnity.Complex","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Roots"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.ratio_eval_eq_of_mul_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.ratio_eval_eq_of_mul_eq","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PolynomialShadowRigidity.lean#L156-L164","doc":"The cross-multiplied identity transports the value to any point where both\ndenominators are nonzero — the substrate anchor included. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.mul_eq_of_ratio_agree_on_rootsOfUnity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.mul_eq_of_ratio_agree_on_rootsOfUnity","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PolynomialShadowRigidity.lean#L139-L154","doc":"**Rigidity for a ratio**, which is the shape `F_λ = [mark]²_q / D(q)²` has.\nAgreement of the two quotients at every root of unity (where the denominators are\nnonzero) forces the cross-multiplied polynomial identity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.eval_eq_of_agree_on_rootsOfUnity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.eval_eq_of_agree_on_rootsOfUnity","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PolynomialShadowRigidity.lean#L130-L137","doc":"**The value is forced — unconditionally.** If two polynomial shadows carry the\nsame root-of-unity data then they take the same value at *every* point, in\nparticular at the substrate anchor `q₀`. This is the honest analogue of\n`q0_value_unique`: same conclusion, no class hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.eq_of_eval_eq_on_rootsOfUnity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.eq_of_eval_eq_on_rootsOfUnity","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PolynomialShadowRigidity.lean#L120-L128","doc":"**Rigidity of the polynomial shadow.** Two polynomials over `ℂ` that agree at\nevery root of unity are equal. Contrast `HabiroRigidity.root_injective`, which\nasserts the analogous statement for the Habiro ring as an unproved hypothesis:\nhere it is a theorem, because the carrier is a polynomial ring and the roots of\nunity are an infinite test set. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.setOf_isRootOfUnity_infinite","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.setOf_isRootOfUnity_infinite","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PolynomialShadowRigidity.lean#L113-L118","doc":"**The roots of unity in `ℂ` are infinite.** This is the input that makes the\nrigidity below elementary: a polynomial identity may be tested on them. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.IsRootOfUnity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Keystone.IsRootOfUnity","kind":"def","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PolynomialShadowRigidity.lean#L93-L94","doc":"`z` is a root of unity: some strictly positive power of it is `1`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}